1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,787
On the second of June,

2
00:00:01,787 --> 00:00:05,932
nineteen eighty-three, a burning airliner landed in

3
00:00:05,932 --> 00:00:06,826
Cincinnati.

4
00:00:07,006 --> 00:00:08,404
The doors opened,

5
00:00:08,404 --> 00:00:10,049
fresh air rushed in,

6
00:00:10,049 --> 00:00:13,092
and in about ninety seconds the cabin

7
00:00:13,092 --> 00:00:14,820
exploded into flames.

8
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:16,869
Twenty-three people died.

9
00:00:17,049 --> 00:00:19,884
Almost everything you will see in this

10
00:00:19,884 --> 00:00:22,869
video exists because of nights like that

11
00:00:22,869 --> 00:00:23,167
one.

12
00:00:23,347 --> 00:00:26,299
Ten small things on every airliner,

13
00:00:26,299 --> 00:00:29,503
things you walk past without noticing,

14
00:00:29,503 --> 00:00:32,370
each one written into the rules by

15
00:00:32,370 --> 00:00:33,297
a disaster.

16
00:00:33,477 --> 00:00:35,587
And number five burns at more than

17
00:00:35,587 --> 00:00:36,890
four hundred degrees.

18
00:00:37,240 --> 00:00:40,284
The plane in Cincinnati was Air Canada

19
00:00:40,284 --> 00:00:42,367
flight seven ninety-seven,

20
00:00:42,367 --> 00:00:45,731
a McDonnell Douglas DC nine with forty-six

21
00:00:45,731 --> 00:00:47,013
people on board,

22
00:00:47,013 --> 00:00:50,778
flying from Dallas toward Toronto and Montreal.

23
00:00:50,958 --> 00:00:53,199
A fire started in or around the

24
00:00:53,199 --> 00:00:56,092
rear lavatory and spread behind the wall

25
00:00:56,092 --> 00:00:58,551
panels, where nobody could see it.

26
00:00:58,731 --> 00:01:00,914
By the time the pilots landed,

27
00:01:00,914 --> 00:01:03,317
the cabin was filling with smoke.

28
00:01:03,497 --> 00:01:05,557
Half the people on board never made

29
00:01:05,557 --> 00:01:05,970
it out.

30
00:01:06,319 --> 00:01:09,301
What came next changed every airliner in

31
00:01:09,301 --> 00:01:10,046
the world.

32
00:01:10,226 --> 00:01:14,766
The investigators and the Federal Aviation Administration

33
00:01:14,766 --> 00:01:17,474
went after every way that fire had

34
00:01:17,474 --> 00:01:18,669
won, the smoke,

35
00:01:18,669 --> 00:01:20,899
the darkness, the materials,

36
00:01:20,899 --> 00:01:21,616
the time.

37
00:01:21,796 --> 00:01:24,818
Every fact in this video comes from

38
00:01:24,818 --> 00:01:29,135
the FAA, the National Transportation Safety Board,

39
00:01:29,135 --> 00:01:31,812
or a named source listed in the

40
00:01:31,812 --> 00:01:32,848
description.

41
00:01:33,028 --> 00:01:35,299
So, look around your next cabin,

42
00:01:35,299 --> 00:01:38,137
and you will find their work everywhere.

43
00:01:38,486 --> 00:01:40,550
Why are there strips of light running

44
00:01:40,550 --> 00:01:42,445
along the floor of every airliner?

45
00:01:42,625 --> 00:01:44,437
Because smoke rises.

46
00:01:44,617 --> 00:01:45,884
In a cabin fire,

47
00:01:45,884 --> 00:01:48,181
the ceiling disappears first,

48
00:01:48,181 --> 00:01:50,874
and the exit signs above your head

49
00:01:50,874 --> 00:01:52,616
can vanish in seconds.

50
00:01:52,796 --> 00:01:54,232
But near the floor,

51
00:01:54,232 --> 00:01:56,501
where people crouch and crawl,

52
00:01:56,501 --> 00:01:58,996
the air stays clearer for longer.

53
00:01:59,176 --> 00:02:02,055
These strips lead you along the aisle

54
00:02:02,055 --> 00:02:03,066
to the exits,

55
00:02:03,066 --> 00:02:05,712
and they change color at the door.

56
00:02:05,892 --> 00:02:09,292
After Air Canada flight seven ninety-seven,

57
00:02:09,292 --> 00:02:12,217
the whole US fleet was retrofitted by

58
00:02:12,217 --> 00:02:13,798
nineteen eighty-six.

59
00:02:13,978 --> 00:02:17,678
The FAA estimated a twenty percent faster

60
00:02:17,678 --> 00:02:20,926
evacuation when the cabin is full of

61
00:02:20,926 --> 00:02:21,467
smoke.

62
00:02:21,817 --> 00:02:24,315
Many of these strips no longer need

63
00:02:24,315 --> 00:02:25,671
electricity at all.

64
00:02:25,851 --> 00:02:28,798
They are made of photoluminescent material,

65
00:02:28,798 --> 00:02:31,333
which soaks up the cabin light during

66
00:02:31,333 --> 00:02:33,458
the flight and glows on its own

67
00:02:33,458 --> 00:02:34,280
in the dark.

68
00:02:34,460 --> 00:02:37,674
So even if the airplane loses power,

69
00:02:37,674 --> 00:02:39,638
the path still shines.

70
00:02:39,818 --> 00:02:43,603
The Flight Safety Foundation says that passengers

71
00:02:43,603 --> 00:02:46,538
can lose up to eighty-three percent of

72
00:02:46,538 --> 00:02:49,936
their wayfinding ability when smoke or fumes

73
00:02:49,936 --> 00:02:50,940
fill a cabin.

74
00:02:51,120 --> 00:02:53,497
The floor path is the answer to

75
00:02:53,497 --> 00:02:54,418
that number.

76
00:02:54,768 --> 00:02:56,941
This is also why the crew dims

77
00:02:56,941 --> 00:02:59,839
the cabin lights for takeoff and landing

78
00:02:59,839 --> 00:03:00,491
at night.

79
00:03:00,671 --> 00:03:05,052
Airline pilot Patrick Smith explains that pre-adjusting

80
00:03:05,052 --> 00:03:07,522
your eyes means you will not be

81
00:03:07,522 --> 00:03:11,026
suddenly blinded while dashing for the doors

82
00:03:11,026 --> 00:03:12,699
in darkness or smoke.

83
00:03:12,879 --> 00:03:15,043
And with dim lights inside,

84
00:03:15,043 --> 00:03:17,368
the crew can also see fire or

85
00:03:17,368 --> 00:03:19,532
debris outside the windows.

86
00:03:19,712 --> 00:03:22,254
The dark cabin is not about mood.

87
00:03:22,434 --> 00:03:25,055
It is about the first ninety seconds

88
00:03:25,055 --> 00:03:26,219
of an emergency.

89
00:03:26,570 --> 00:03:29,013
Why is there a smoke detector on

90
00:03:29,013 --> 00:03:31,991
the ceiling of every airplane bathroom?

91
00:03:32,171 --> 00:03:35,078
Because a lavatory is a small closed

92
00:03:35,078 --> 00:03:37,500
room where nobody is watching.

93
00:03:37,680 --> 00:03:40,599
On Air Canada flight seven ninety-seven,

94
00:03:40,599 --> 00:03:43,007
the fire started in or around the

95
00:03:43,007 --> 00:03:46,072
rear lavatory, and it was spreading behind

96
00:03:46,072 --> 00:03:49,209
the panels before anyone understood how bad

97
00:03:49,209 --> 00:03:49,720
it was.

98
00:03:49,900 --> 00:03:52,063
Minutes matter in a fire at thirty

99
00:03:52,063 --> 00:03:54,736
thousand feet, because there is nowhere to

100
00:03:54,736 --> 00:03:54,927
go.

101
00:03:55,107 --> 00:03:56,695
After that flight,

102
00:03:56,695 --> 00:04:00,401
lavatory smoke detectors became mandatory.

103
00:04:00,581 --> 00:04:04,594
The FAA required them in nineteen eighty-six,

104
00:04:04,594 --> 00:04:08,518
and gave airlines eighteen months to install

105
00:04:08,518 --> 00:04:08,964
them.

106
00:04:09,314 --> 00:04:11,299
The detector does two jobs.

107
00:04:11,479 --> 00:04:13,179
It warns the crew early,

108
00:04:13,179 --> 00:04:15,729
while the fire is still small enough

109
00:04:15,729 --> 00:04:18,422
to fight with a handheld extinguisher.

110
00:04:18,602 --> 00:04:20,602
And it stops a lot of fires

111
00:04:20,602 --> 00:04:21,936
before they start,

112
00:04:21,936 --> 00:04:24,603
because it catches people who try to

113
00:04:24,603 --> 00:04:25,788
smoke in secret.

114
00:04:25,968 --> 00:04:28,533
Tampering with it is a serious federal

115
00:04:28,533 --> 00:04:30,490
offense in the United States,

116
00:04:30,490 --> 00:04:32,649
and the warning sign on the door

117
00:04:32,649 --> 00:04:33,526
tells you so.

118
00:04:33,706 --> 00:04:36,181
That little dome is watching on behalf

119
00:04:36,181 --> 00:04:37,549
of everyone on board.

120
00:04:37,899 --> 00:04:39,500
In the same period,

121
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:43,123
the FAA also added Halon fire extinguishers

122
00:04:43,123 --> 00:04:47,504
to every commercial airliner in nineteen eighty-six,

123
00:04:47,504 --> 00:04:51,043
with two required per aircraft in addition

124
00:04:51,043 --> 00:04:53,318
to the other extinguishers.

125
00:04:53,498 --> 00:04:56,118
Halon smothers a fire chemically,

126
00:04:56,118 --> 00:04:58,262
without the mess of powder,

127
00:04:58,262 --> 00:05:01,121
which matters in a sealed cabin full

128
00:05:01,121 --> 00:05:01,915
of people.

129
00:05:02,095 --> 00:05:04,196
Detection, then suppression.

130
00:05:04,376 --> 00:05:06,333
That is the playbook that came out

131
00:05:06,333 --> 00:05:07,139
of Cincinnati.

132
00:05:07,490 --> 00:05:09,579
Why does the trash bin in an

133
00:05:09,579 --> 00:05:13,236
airplane bathroom have its own fire extinguisher?

134
00:05:13,416 --> 00:05:15,951
Because it is the most likely place

135
00:05:15,951 --> 00:05:17,399
for a fire to start.

136
00:05:17,579 --> 00:05:19,387
It is full of paper towels,

137
00:05:19,387 --> 00:05:21,866
and it is exactly where someone might

138
00:05:21,866 --> 00:05:24,545
throw a cigarette they were not supposed

139
00:05:24,545 --> 00:05:25,148
to light.

140
00:05:25,328 --> 00:05:27,678
So hidden above the bin is a

141
00:05:27,678 --> 00:05:30,112
small automatic extinguisher.

142
00:05:30,292 --> 00:05:32,021
When the heat rises,

143
00:05:32,021 --> 00:05:33,664
it fires by itself,

144
00:05:33,664 --> 00:05:35,307
and floods the bin.

145
00:05:35,487 --> 00:05:37,577
No crew member has to notice.

146
00:05:37,757 --> 00:05:41,304
The FAA made these automatic bin extinguishers

147
00:05:41,304 --> 00:05:44,003
mandatory in nineteen eighty-seven,

148
00:05:44,003 --> 00:05:45,930
with a two-year deadline.

149
00:05:46,281 --> 00:05:48,701
The flap on top of the bin

150
00:05:48,701 --> 00:05:51,029
is part of the same idea.

151
00:05:51,209 --> 00:05:54,637
It springs closed after you push something

152
00:05:54,637 --> 00:05:57,575
through, so a fire inside starves of

153
00:05:57,575 --> 00:05:59,533
air and stays contained.

154
00:05:59,713 --> 00:06:01,972
And this is the same reason the

155
00:06:01,972 --> 00:06:04,157
bathroom still has an ashtray,

156
00:06:04,157 --> 00:06:06,343
even though smoking is banned.

157
00:06:06,523 --> 00:06:08,765
The rules assume that someone,

158
00:06:08,765 --> 00:06:11,007
someday, will break the rules,

159
00:06:11,007 --> 00:06:13,399
and they give that person a safe

160
00:06:13,399 --> 00:06:15,716
place to put out the cigarette,

161
00:06:15,716 --> 00:06:17,285
instead of the trash.

162
00:06:17,635 --> 00:06:20,525
Think about why automatic matters here.

163
00:06:20,705 --> 00:06:23,766
A lavatory fire often starts when the

164
00:06:23,766 --> 00:06:26,743
door is closed and nobody is inside,

165
00:06:26,743 --> 00:06:29,473
or when the only person inside is

166
00:06:29,473 --> 00:06:31,375
the one who started it.

167
00:06:31,555 --> 00:06:34,076
A crew member might not walk past

168
00:06:34,076 --> 00:06:35,375
for many minutes.

169
00:06:35,555 --> 00:06:38,981
The bin extinguisher does not need anyone

170
00:06:38,981 --> 00:06:40,485
to notice, decide,

171
00:06:40,485 --> 00:06:41,069
or act.

172
00:06:41,249 --> 00:06:44,002
It turns the most dangerous corner of

173
00:06:44,002 --> 00:06:46,903
the cabin into one that defends itself.

174
00:06:47,254 --> 00:06:49,947
Why is the cushion of your airplane

175
00:06:49,947 --> 00:06:51,178
seat so special?

176
00:06:51,358 --> 00:06:53,366
Because it is fire blocked.

177
00:06:53,546 --> 00:06:56,862
Ordinary foam burns fast and releases thick

178
00:06:56,862 --> 00:06:59,407
toxic smoke, and in a cabin fire,

179
00:06:59,407 --> 00:07:02,106
the seats are the biggest fuel load

180
00:07:02,106 --> 00:07:03,032
in the room.

181
00:07:03,212 --> 00:07:05,761
In October nineteen eighty-four,

182
00:07:05,761 --> 00:07:07,992
the FAA set a tough new test

183
00:07:07,992 --> 00:07:09,426
for seat cushions,

184
00:07:09,426 --> 00:07:11,339
using a kerosene burner.

185
00:07:11,519 --> 00:07:14,877
The airlines replaced six hundred fifty thousand

186
00:07:14,877 --> 00:07:16,906
cushions across the US fleet.

187
00:07:17,086 --> 00:07:20,355
In tests, the new cushions gave passengers

188
00:07:20,355 --> 00:07:23,157
forty to sixty seconds of extra time

189
00:07:23,157 --> 00:07:23,936
to escape.

190
00:07:24,116 --> 00:07:24,805
In a fire,

191
00:07:24,805 --> 00:07:27,703
forty seconds can be the whole difference.

192
00:07:28,053 --> 00:07:30,412
The walls and ceiling got the same

193
00:07:30,412 --> 00:07:31,106
treatment.

194
00:07:31,286 --> 00:07:35,262
A nineteen eighty-five standard targeted the big

195
00:07:35,262 --> 00:07:37,002
panels, the ceilings,

196
00:07:37,002 --> 00:07:40,232
the walls, the galleys and the overhead

197
00:07:40,232 --> 00:07:44,209
bins, to delay what firefighters call flashover,

198
00:07:44,209 --> 00:07:47,191
the moment when a whole room ignites

199
00:07:47,191 --> 00:07:47,853
at once.

200
00:07:48,033 --> 00:07:51,830
Airplanes built after August nineteen ninety had

201
00:07:51,830 --> 00:07:53,254
to meet the final,

202
00:07:53,254 --> 00:07:55,232
lower heat release level.

203
00:07:55,412 --> 00:07:57,958
That is the exact thing that killed

204
00:07:57,958 --> 00:08:00,067
so many people in Cincinnati.

205
00:08:00,418 --> 00:08:01,648
Notice the pattern.

206
00:08:01,828 --> 00:08:04,475
Nobody could promise that a fire would

207
00:08:04,475 --> 00:08:05,381
never happen.

208
00:08:05,561 --> 00:08:08,196
So engineers attacked time.

209
00:08:08,376 --> 00:08:10,559
Lights that show the way out.

210
00:08:10,739 --> 00:08:12,713
Detectors that warn early.

211
00:08:12,893 --> 00:08:16,120
Extinguishers that fire by themselves.

212
00:08:16,300 --> 00:08:18,483
Materials that burn slower.

213
00:08:18,663 --> 00:08:20,352
Each one adds seconds,

214
00:08:20,352 --> 00:08:23,731
and together they turn an unsurvivable cabin

215
00:08:23,731 --> 00:08:26,418
into one where people can walk out.

216
00:08:26,598 --> 00:08:28,998
Which brings us to the most famous

217
00:08:28,998 --> 00:08:31,115
object in the safety briefing.

218
00:08:31,465 --> 00:08:33,868
Where does the oxygen in the drop

219
00:08:33,868 --> 00:08:35,981
down mask actually come from?

220
00:08:36,161 --> 00:08:38,600
There is no oxygen tank above your

221
00:08:38,600 --> 00:08:38,959
seat.

222
00:08:39,139 --> 00:08:41,066
There is a chemical generator.

223
00:08:41,246 --> 00:08:43,205
When you pull the mask,

224
00:08:43,205 --> 00:08:45,846
a spring fires a small cap that

225
00:08:45,846 --> 00:08:49,169
ignites a core of sodium chlorate mixed

226
00:08:49,169 --> 00:08:51,469
with iron, and as it burns,

227
00:08:51,469 --> 00:08:54,110
it releases almost pure oxygen.

228
00:08:54,290 --> 00:08:55,543
Once it starts,

229
00:08:55,543 --> 00:08:57,297
it cannot be stopped.

230
00:08:57,477 --> 00:08:59,726
It runs for about twelve to twenty

231
00:08:59,726 --> 00:09:02,240
minutes, enough for the pilots to dive

232
00:09:02,240 --> 00:09:04,423
down to around ten thousand feet,

233
00:09:04,423 --> 00:09:06,672
where you can breathe on your own.

234
00:09:06,852 --> 00:09:08,968
And the bag may not inflate even

235
00:09:08,968 --> 00:09:10,753
when the oxygen is flowing.

236
00:09:11,103 --> 00:09:12,205
Why the hurry?

237
00:09:12,385 --> 00:09:14,820
Because at forty thousand feet,

238
00:09:14,820 --> 00:09:17,647
the FAA estimates the time of useful

239
00:09:17,647 --> 00:09:20,710
consciousness at less than ten seconds.

240
00:09:20,890 --> 00:09:23,178
That is why the briefing tells you

241
00:09:23,178 --> 00:09:25,197
to put your own mask on first.

242
00:09:25,377 --> 00:09:27,479
And the generator gets hot.

243
00:09:27,659 --> 00:09:30,831
Its case typically reaches four hundred fifty

244
00:09:30,831 --> 00:09:33,299
to five hundred degrees Fahrenheit,

245
00:09:33,299 --> 00:09:36,401
and passengers often notice a burning smell.

246
00:09:36,581 --> 00:09:38,334
That smell is normal.

247
00:09:38,514 --> 00:09:41,033
It is the chemistry doing its job.

248
00:09:41,384 --> 00:09:43,508
That heat has a dark side.

249
00:09:43,688 --> 00:09:45,646
On the eleventh of May,

250
00:09:45,646 --> 00:09:50,670
nineteen ninety-six, ValuJet flight five ninety-two crashed

251
00:09:50,670 --> 00:09:53,054
into the Florida Everglades,

252
00:09:53,054 --> 00:09:56,204
killing all one hundred ten people on

253
00:09:56,204 --> 00:09:56,715
board.

254
00:09:56,895 --> 00:09:58,973
Expired oxygen generators,

255
00:09:58,973 --> 00:10:02,331
wrongly labeled as empty and missing their

256
00:10:02,331 --> 00:10:05,129
safety caps, had been loaded in the

257
00:10:05,129 --> 00:10:06,008
cargo hold.

258
00:10:06,188 --> 00:10:09,351
They ignited, and because they make their

259
00:10:09,351 --> 00:10:11,819
own oxygen, the fire fed itself.

260
00:10:11,999 --> 00:10:15,376
After that, these generators were banned as

261
00:10:15,376 --> 00:10:17,653
cargo on passenger airplanes.

262
00:10:18,004 --> 00:10:20,887
Why is a thin airplane seat built

263
00:10:20,887 --> 00:10:24,120
to survive sixteen times the force of

264
00:10:24,120 --> 00:10:24,819
gravity?

265
00:10:24,999 --> 00:10:28,574
Because most airplane accidents are survivable,

266
00:10:28,574 --> 00:10:31,160
if your seat stays attached to the

267
00:10:31,160 --> 00:10:31,616
floor.

268
00:10:31,796 --> 00:10:34,875
For decades, seats were only approved to

269
00:10:34,875 --> 00:10:37,030
a static standard of nine G,

270
00:10:37,030 --> 00:10:39,494
with no test of what happened to

271
00:10:39,494 --> 00:10:41,572
the person sitting in them.

272
00:10:41,752 --> 00:10:43,970
In nineteen eighty-eight,

273
00:10:43,970 --> 00:10:47,875
the FAA required newly designed airliners to

274
00:10:47,875 --> 00:10:49,650
use sixteen G seats,

275
00:10:49,650 --> 00:10:53,465
tested dynamically with a crash test dummy,

276
00:10:53,465 --> 00:10:54,442
like a car.

277
00:10:54,622 --> 00:10:58,430
And airplanes built after October twenty-seventh,

278
00:10:58,430 --> 00:10:59,830
two thousand nine,

279
00:10:59,830 --> 00:11:02,472
had to meet the sixteen G standard

280
00:11:02,472 --> 00:11:02,783
too.

281
00:11:03,134 --> 00:11:04,875
The difference is huge.

282
00:11:05,055 --> 00:11:07,568
A static test just pulls on a

283
00:11:07,568 --> 00:11:08,608
seat slowly.

284
00:11:08,788 --> 00:11:11,493
A dynamic test fires it down a

285
00:11:11,493 --> 00:11:14,288
sled at high speed and stops it

286
00:11:14,288 --> 00:11:18,436
violently, and measures what the dummy's head,

287
00:11:18,436 --> 00:11:20,781
spine and legs experience.

288
00:11:20,961 --> 00:11:23,624
A seat that tears loose turns into

289
00:11:23,624 --> 00:11:26,445
a projectile, and it also blocks the

290
00:11:26,445 --> 00:11:29,657
aisle that everyone else needs to escape.

291
00:11:29,837 --> 00:11:32,183
A seat that holds keeps you in

292
00:11:32,183 --> 00:11:34,608
place, and keeps the path open.

293
00:11:34,959 --> 00:11:36,619
And it is not only the seat.

294
00:11:36,799 --> 00:11:40,203
The same logic reaches the flight attendants'

295
00:11:40,203 --> 00:11:42,245
jump seats, the seat belts,

296
00:11:42,245 --> 00:11:45,120
and the way overhead bins are latched,

297
00:11:45,120 --> 00:11:47,995
because anything that comes loose in a

298
00:11:47,995 --> 00:11:51,172
crash becomes something that hurts people.

299
00:11:51,352 --> 00:11:53,771
The goal is simple to say and

300
00:11:53,771 --> 00:11:54,939
hard to build.

301
00:11:55,119 --> 00:11:57,063
When the airplane stops,

302
00:11:57,063 --> 00:11:59,815
the cabin should still look like a

303
00:11:59,815 --> 00:12:02,649
cabin, with every seat in place and

304
00:12:02,649 --> 00:12:04,025
every aisle open,

305
00:12:04,025 --> 00:12:06,697
so that everyone can stand up and

306
00:12:06,697 --> 00:12:07,426
walk out.

307
00:12:07,776 --> 00:12:10,489
Why is the bottom of every airplane

308
00:12:10,489 --> 00:12:12,350
door so thick and heavy?

309
00:12:12,530 --> 00:12:16,006
Because there is an inflatable slide packed

310
00:12:16,006 --> 00:12:16,814
inside it.

311
00:12:16,994 --> 00:12:19,765
Open the door in an emergency,

312
00:12:19,765 --> 00:12:22,998
and the slide inflates in about six

313
00:12:22,998 --> 00:12:25,400
seconds, using stored gas.

314
00:12:25,580 --> 00:12:26,430
It has to,

315
00:12:26,430 --> 00:12:29,493
because of one of the toughest rules

316
00:12:29,493 --> 00:12:30,514
in aviation.

317
00:12:30,694 --> 00:12:32,013
To be certified,

318
00:12:32,013 --> 00:12:35,229
an airliner must prove that everyone on

319
00:12:35,229 --> 00:12:38,609
board can be evacuated in ninety seconds,

320
00:12:38,609 --> 00:12:40,753
using only half the exits,

321
00:12:40,753 --> 00:12:43,639
with a realistic mix of passengers.

322
00:12:43,819 --> 00:12:46,702
Ninety seconds is roughly how long the

323
00:12:46,702 --> 00:12:49,888
cabin of Air Canada seven ninety-seven had

324
00:12:49,888 --> 00:12:51,632
after the doors opened.

325
00:12:51,983 --> 00:12:54,659
On airplanes that fly over water,

326
00:12:54,659 --> 00:12:57,660
many of those slides are slide rafts.

327
00:12:57,840 --> 00:12:59,547
After everyone is out,

328
00:12:59,547 --> 00:13:01,488
the crew can detach them,

329
00:13:01,488 --> 00:13:04,282
and they float as life rafts holding

330
00:13:04,282 --> 00:13:06,222
thirty to seventy people.

331
00:13:06,402 --> 00:13:08,779
So the same object that gets you

332
00:13:08,779 --> 00:13:10,785
down to the runway in a few

333
00:13:10,785 --> 00:13:13,310
seconds can also keep you alive in

334
00:13:13,310 --> 00:13:14,053
the ocean.

335
00:13:14,233 --> 00:13:16,479
It is one of the most cleverly

336
00:13:16,479 --> 00:13:19,400
packed pieces of equipment in aviation.

337
00:13:19,750 --> 00:13:22,164
The ninety second test is brutal.

338
00:13:22,344 --> 00:13:25,603
Manufacturers fill a cabin with volunteers,

339
00:13:25,603 --> 00:13:27,421
block half of the exits,

340
00:13:27,421 --> 00:13:29,467
turn off the normal lights,

341
00:13:29,467 --> 00:13:30,982
and start the clock.

342
00:13:31,162 --> 00:13:33,718
Only the floor lighting and the emergency

343
00:13:33,718 --> 00:13:35,713
lights help people find the way.

344
00:13:35,893 --> 00:13:38,284
Everything in this video meets in that

345
00:13:38,284 --> 00:13:38,598
test.

346
00:13:38,778 --> 00:13:41,560
The lights, the seats that stayed in

347
00:13:41,560 --> 00:13:44,806
place, the slides that inflate in seconds,

348
00:13:44,806 --> 00:13:47,510
and the crew who knows exactly what

349
00:13:47,510 --> 00:13:48,206
to shout.

350
00:13:48,386 --> 00:13:51,071
If the airplane cannot empty in time,

351
00:13:51,071 --> 00:13:52,740
it cannot be certified.

352
00:13:53,090 --> 00:13:55,173
What does it mean when the crew

353
00:13:55,173 --> 00:13:57,525
says arm the doors and cross check?

354
00:13:57,705 --> 00:14:00,521
Arming a door connects the packed slide

355
00:14:00,521 --> 00:14:02,616
to the floor of the airplane.

356
00:14:02,796 --> 00:14:05,386
If an armed door is opened,

357
00:14:05,386 --> 00:14:09,991
the slide blasts out and inflates automatically,

358
00:14:09,991 --> 00:14:11,909
with no extra steps,

359
00:14:11,909 --> 00:14:14,212
because in an emergency,

360
00:14:14,212 --> 00:14:17,378
there is no time for extra steps.

361
00:14:17,558 --> 00:14:20,834
Cross check means a second flight attendant

362
00:14:20,834 --> 00:14:22,434
confirms it was done.

363
00:14:22,614 --> 00:14:25,004
Before the jet bridge comes back at

364
00:14:25,004 --> 00:14:27,257
the gate, the doors are disarmed,

365
00:14:27,257 --> 00:14:29,579
so the slide does not explode onto

366
00:14:29,579 --> 00:14:30,671
the ground crew.

367
00:14:30,851 --> 00:14:32,831
It is a tiny ritual hiding a

368
00:14:32,831 --> 00:14:34,032
powerful machine.

369
00:14:34,383 --> 00:14:37,160
That is also why the crew watches

370
00:14:37,160 --> 00:14:39,096
that door so carefully.

371
00:14:39,276 --> 00:14:42,064
A slide deployed by accident at the

372
00:14:42,064 --> 00:14:44,454
gate is not just embarrassing.

373
00:14:44,634 --> 00:14:46,434
It can injure someone,

374
00:14:46,434 --> 00:14:49,378
it takes the airplane out of service

375
00:14:49,378 --> 00:14:52,650
until the slide is repacked or replaced,

376
00:14:52,650 --> 00:14:55,594
and it costs the airline real money.

377
00:14:55,774 --> 00:14:58,516
So the arming lever is designed to

378
00:14:58,516 --> 00:15:00,371
be deliberate, colored,

379
00:15:00,371 --> 00:15:01,823
and checked twice.

380
00:15:02,003 --> 00:15:05,064
The cross check exists because one person

381
00:15:05,064 --> 00:15:06,482
can make a mistake,

382
00:15:06,482 --> 00:15:08,946
and two rarely make the same one.

383
00:15:09,296 --> 00:15:11,809
Why does the safety briefing tell you

384
00:15:11,809 --> 00:15:14,187
not to inflate your life vest until

385
00:15:14,187 --> 00:15:16,157
you are outside the airplane?

386
00:15:16,337 --> 00:15:17,870
Because it can trap you.

387
00:15:18,050 --> 00:15:20,707
On the twenty-third of November,

388
00:15:20,707 --> 00:15:25,440
nineteen ninety-six, a hijacked Ethiopian Airlines Boeing

389
00:15:25,440 --> 00:15:28,512
seven sixty-seven ran out of fuel and

390
00:15:28,512 --> 00:15:31,584
ditched in the ocean near the Comoros

391
00:15:31,584 --> 00:15:32,249
Islands.

392
00:15:32,429 --> 00:15:35,303
Some passengers survived the impact,

393
00:15:35,303 --> 00:15:38,736
but had already inflated their vests inside

394
00:15:38,736 --> 00:15:39,534
the cabin.

395
00:15:39,714 --> 00:15:41,127
As water rushed in,

396
00:15:41,127 --> 00:15:44,176
the vests lifted them toward the ceiling,

397
00:15:44,176 --> 00:15:46,482
and they could not swim down to

398
00:15:46,482 --> 00:15:47,226
the exits.

399
00:15:47,406 --> 00:15:49,797
They drowned inside the airplane.

400
00:15:49,977 --> 00:15:52,647
The instruction exists because of them.

401
00:15:52,998 --> 00:15:56,316
Of the one hundred seventy-five people on

402
00:15:56,316 --> 00:15:57,772
board that flight,

403
00:15:57,772 --> 00:16:00,119
one hundred twenty-five died,

404
00:16:00,119 --> 00:16:02,547
including the three hijackers,

405
00:16:02,547 --> 00:16:04,085
and fifty survived.

406
00:16:04,265 --> 00:16:07,101
The pilots managed to put the airplane

407
00:16:07,101 --> 00:16:09,639
down in shallow water off a beach,

408
00:16:09,639 --> 00:16:12,102
which is part of why anyone lived

409
00:16:12,102 --> 00:16:12,624
at all.

410
00:16:12,804 --> 00:16:14,816
So when you hear that line in

411
00:16:14,816 --> 00:16:17,383
the briefing, do not inflate the vest

412
00:16:17,383 --> 00:16:18,770
inside the aircraft,

413
00:16:18,770 --> 00:16:21,268
remember that it is not a formality.

414
00:16:21,448 --> 00:16:22,504
It is a lesson.

415
00:16:22,855 --> 00:16:26,019
The vest has other small details with

416
00:16:26,019 --> 00:16:27,046
big reasons.

417
00:16:27,226 --> 00:16:29,664
A light that switches on in water,

418
00:16:29,664 --> 00:16:32,102
so rescuers can find you at night.

419
00:16:32,282 --> 00:16:35,282
A whistle, because a voice gets tired

420
00:16:35,282 --> 00:16:37,065
and a whistle carries.

421
00:16:37,245 --> 00:16:39,535
And manual tubes to top up the

422
00:16:39,535 --> 00:16:41,901
air if the gas cartridge fails.

423
00:16:42,081 --> 00:16:44,804
Every part of that yellow vest assumes

424
00:16:44,804 --> 00:16:45,735
you are cold,

425
00:16:45,735 --> 00:16:46,595
in the dark,

426
00:16:46,595 --> 00:16:49,175
and waiting for someone to find you.

427
00:16:49,525 --> 00:16:51,523
Why is the cockpit door on a

428
00:16:51,523 --> 00:16:53,379
passenger jet bulletproof?

429
00:16:53,559 --> 00:16:55,364
Before two thousand one,

430
00:16:55,364 --> 00:16:58,071
cockpit doors were light and easy to

431
00:16:58,071 --> 00:16:58,447
open.

432
00:16:58,627 --> 00:17:01,426
After the September eleventh attacks,

433
00:17:01,426 --> 00:17:05,058
airlines first reinforced their doors with metal

434
00:17:05,058 --> 00:17:06,193
bars, and then,

435
00:17:06,193 --> 00:17:08,311
by April two thousand three,

436
00:17:08,311 --> 00:17:11,716
every large commercial airplane in the United

437
00:17:11,716 --> 00:17:14,591
States had a bulletproof cockpit door.

438
00:17:14,771 --> 00:17:16,884
It stays locked during flight.

439
00:17:17,064 --> 00:17:19,780
The pilots control who comes in.

440
00:17:19,960 --> 00:17:21,856
It is the one door on the

441
00:17:21,856 --> 00:17:24,660
airplane designed to keep people out,

442
00:17:24,660 --> 00:17:26,404
not to let them escape.

443
00:17:26,754 --> 00:17:29,660
But a locked door creates new problems,

444
00:17:29,660 --> 00:17:32,268
and engineers and crews have had to

445
00:17:32,268 --> 00:17:33,162
manage them.

446
00:17:33,342 --> 00:17:36,241
Pilots still need to leave for the

447
00:17:36,241 --> 00:17:39,565
restroom or swap crews on long flights.

448
00:17:39,745 --> 00:17:41,750
As one pilot put it in two

449
00:17:41,750 --> 00:17:44,834
thousand three, it's a barrier when it's

450
00:17:44,834 --> 00:17:47,686
closed, it's an entry when it's open.

451
00:17:47,866 --> 00:17:49,827
That is why you will often see

452
00:17:49,827 --> 00:17:52,246
a cart or a flight attendant blocking

453
00:17:52,246 --> 00:17:54,403
the aisle while the door is open.

454
00:17:54,583 --> 00:17:57,412
Every safety device has a weak moment,

455
00:17:57,412 --> 00:18:00,167
and the procedures exist to cover it.

456
00:18:00,518 --> 00:18:02,551
There is a lesson here that goes

457
00:18:02,551 --> 00:18:04,012
beyond the door itself.

458
00:18:04,192 --> 00:18:06,899
Safety on an airplane is a balance

459
00:18:06,899 --> 00:18:10,322
between getting people out fast and keeping

460
00:18:10,322 --> 00:18:11,994
the wrong people out.

461
00:18:12,174 --> 00:18:14,043
Every exit, every lock,

462
00:18:14,043 --> 00:18:17,132
and every rule is a compromise between

463
00:18:17,132 --> 00:18:18,432
those two goals.

464
00:18:18,612 --> 00:18:21,538
The cockpit door is the clearest example,

465
00:18:21,538 --> 00:18:23,893
because it was built for a threat

466
00:18:23,893 --> 00:18:26,819
that the people who designed the original

467
00:18:26,819 --> 00:18:28,318
cabin never imagined.

468
00:18:28,668 --> 00:18:30,142
So here are the ten.

469
00:18:30,322 --> 00:18:32,939
Lights on the floor that glow without

470
00:18:32,939 --> 00:18:33,364
power.

471
00:18:33,544 --> 00:18:36,328
A detector that watches the bathroom,

472
00:18:36,328 --> 00:18:39,337
and an extinguisher hidden in the trash.

473
00:18:39,517 --> 00:18:42,025
Cushions that buy forty seconds,

474
00:18:42,025 --> 00:18:44,298
a mask that burns to give you

475
00:18:44,298 --> 00:18:46,884
air, and a seat built for sixteen

476
00:18:46,884 --> 00:18:47,040
G.

477
00:18:47,220 --> 00:18:50,151
A slide that inflates in six seconds,

478
00:18:50,151 --> 00:18:52,765
a door ritual that keeps it under

479
00:18:52,765 --> 00:18:55,616
control, a vest you must not inflate

480
00:18:55,616 --> 00:18:58,467
inside, and a door that keeps people

481
00:18:58,467 --> 00:18:58,784
out.

482
00:18:58,964 --> 00:19:01,019
Most of them were paid for by

483
00:19:01,019 --> 00:19:01,727
a tragedy.

484
00:19:02,078 --> 00:19:03,713
The next time you board,

485
00:19:03,713 --> 00:19:04,667
look for them.

486
00:19:04,847 --> 00:19:06,505
Count the floor lights,

487
00:19:06,505 --> 00:19:08,957
find the thick bulge at the bottom

488
00:19:08,957 --> 00:19:11,121
of the door where the slide is

489
00:19:11,121 --> 00:19:13,429
packed, and read the sign on the

490
00:19:13,429 --> 00:19:14,727
bathroom detector.

491
00:19:14,907 --> 00:19:17,779
Then remember that flying is as safe

492
00:19:17,779 --> 00:19:20,491
as it is because people refused to

493
00:19:20,491 --> 00:19:22,964
accept the same accident twice.

